    {
      "id": "F6",
      "statement": "Shared devices on routes, alert 2: no device was used by two or more of its route accounts. A-9895 sent from D-699, and A-1415 sent from D-785. The only other shared device in the dataset, D-619 (used by A-2952 and A-9652), is not on either alert's route.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "X1",
        "S2",
        "E19",
        "E20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F7",
      "statement": "What separates the alerts: both show 6 senders and 6 transfers in one hour, but the recorded relationships beneath them differ. (a) Onward depth: alert 1 has a 3-hop onward route along A-9278, A-5092, A-2897 and A-4989, and all of its contributing transfers lead onward. Alert 2 has a single 1-hop link, and only half of its contributing transfers lead onward. (b) Amounts: on alert 1's route, two batches of credits (141500 and 215500) move on as 138000 and 210000, and each later hop is a few thousand smaller. Alert 2 has uniform 2500 credits, and only 14000 moves on. (c) Devices: every hop on alert 1's route was sent from one device, D-568, by three different route accounts. Alert 2's route has no shared device. (d) Downstream alerts: no downstream route account of either alert (A-5092, A-2897, A-4989, A-1415) has its own fan-in alert. So the indicator alone would miss the route behind alert 1.",
